  <div class="eye">RESEARCH · GUIDE</div>
  <h1 class="page-title">LLM cost visibility <em>for CFOs.</em></h1>
  <p class="updated">A finance guide · 26 July 2026</p>
  <p class="byline">By the LLM CFO team</p>

  <p class="lede">Cost visibility is the foundation of every finance control. If you cannot see the spend, you cannot budget it, optimize it, or explain it. This guide covers the dashboards and cadence that make AI costs board-ready.</p>

  <h2>1. The visibility you need</h2>
  <p>At minimum, finance should be able to answer:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>What did we spend this month vs. last month?</li>
    <li>Which teams and features drove the change?</li>
    <li>Which models and providers are we paying?</li>
    <li>How does actual compare to budget?</li>
    <li>What is our cost per meaningful outcome?</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. The CFO dashboard</h2>
  <p>A useful CFO dashboard has two views: summary and drill-down. The summary view shows trend, variance, and the top three drivers. The drill-down lets you explore by team, feature, model, and environment.</p>
  <p>Keep it simple. A dashboard with fifty charts is a dashboard no one uses. Start with six to eight metrics and add only when someone asks a question you cannot answer.</p>

  <h2>3. Metrics that matter to finance</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Budget vs. actual</strong> — by team and by feature.</li>
    <li><strong>Variance explanation</strong> — volume, price, or mix.</li>
    <li><strong>Cost per task</strong> — links spend to business value.</li>
    <li><strong>Spend concentration</strong> — the Pareto of endpoints.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. The operating rhythm</h2>
  <p>Weekly: review the largest movers with engineering and product. Monthly: reconcile to the invoice and update forecasts. Quarterly: present the board with trend, efficiency, and the pipeline of optimization work.</p>

